  "name": "Taiwanese Mandopop (Taipei ballad industry)",
  "family": "pop",
  "lineage": "Taipei became the centre of Mandarin-language pop after 1980 when Rock Records, UFO and later Sony Music Taiwan built an export industry aimed at the whole Chinese-speaking world — Teresa Teng's soft-voiced ballad style, Lo Ta-yu's songwriter turn and Jonathan Lee's producer-arranger school established the canonical form: 60-90 BPM piano-and-string ballads in 4/4, verse-prechorus-chorus with a modulated final chorus, close-miked breathy chest-voice Mandarin delivery, and from the 1990s an R&B-inflected Jay Chou variant with mumbled syncopated phrasing and pentatonic 'China wind' hooks.",
